import json,pathlib,html
P=pathlib.Path(__file__).resolve().parent
x=json.loads((P/'derived-inputs-and-results.json').read_text());s=json.loads((P/'sources-key.json').read_text())
def ref(k,words=None):return f'<a href="{s[k]["url"]}">{words or s[k]["title"]}</a>'
def table(headers,rows):return '<div class="scroll"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th>'+v+'</th>' for v in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+str(v)+'</td>' for v in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'
def f(v):return 'Not separately verified' if v is None else f'{v:,.1f}'
a=x['annual_and_ttm'];b=x['cases']['base'];R=[]
R.append('<header><p class="eyebrow">Fundamental Pullback · SLGN · 7 November 2026</p><h1>Silgan: cash quality before a transformative deal</h1><p class="lead">Watchlist initiation. No current fair value or entry approval. Opportunity 80 prioritizes research; it does not resolve declining underlying volumes, inventory-accounting benefits or the new financing and minority-interest structure.</p></header>')
R.append('<section><h2>Decision and evidence</h2><p>The central question is whether higher-value dispensing and genuine operating improvements can create durable cash growth after reinvestment, while Trivium financing and the 40% outside ownership leave enough value for existing shareholders. The positive case is a resilient food-packaging franchise and potentially attractive synergy execution. The adverse case is weak volumes, inflated interpretation of adjusted earnings and paying too much for a leveraged integration.</p><p>The scanner reference is $41.09, 17.1% below $49.55, QP 80, Opportunity 80 and RSI 58.5 on November 6. The scanner timestamp has no verified timezone; separately, historical prices verify the November 6 regular-session close at $41.09, 16:00 EST. Matching prices do not establish identical observations. '+ref('quote')+' EMA20/50/200, volume, support, stop and resistance are not available. Research is allowed under the requested ≥65 exploration threshold; that is not a permanent change to the plan’s normal screen or an entry signal.</p><p>Evidence through November 6 includes the November 5 10-Q and November 6 lender consent. Three fiscal years and two comparable TTM periods are reconstructed below. The report is an initial, provisional underwrite, not a completed deal-inclusive valuation.</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>What changed operationally</h2><p>Q3 sales increased 1.6% to $2,040.2m, but reported EBIT declined 3.5% to $191.7m. Dispensing and Specialty Closures volumes were flat overall; fragrance and North American prescription-pharma growth did not offset weaker food/beverage closures. Metal container volumes fell 4%; Custom Containers fell 8%, including about three percentage points from exited low-margin business. Higher steel/resin and manufacturing-cost pass-through increased reported revenue. It should not be modeled as physical demand growth or an automatic improvement in unit economics. '+ref('latest')+'</p><p>Company adjusted EPS rose from $1.22 to $1.31, helped by lower interest expense and a slightly lower diluted share count; reported EPS declined from $1.06 to $0.99. Adjusted EBIT includes an inventory effect: the 10-Q reports LIFO layer liquidation benefits of $26.8m in Q3 and $34.0m year-to-date. Removing only that disclosed Q3 benefit gives $208.198m adjusted EBIT versus prior reported adjusted EBIT of $221.408m, not a fully normalized like-for-like comparison. The prior 10-Q and annual inventory notes do not separately quantify a comparable liquidation benefit; no zero is assumed. '+ref('q3')+'; '+ref('prior')+'</p><p>FY2026 adjusted EPS guidance is $3.83–$3.93 and company-defined FCF approximately $450m, with capital expenditure approximately $310m. These are management forecasts, not consensus or FCFF. The next earnings date was not verified on the readable official calendar; expected transaction closing in mid-2027 remains conditional. '+ref('latest')+'; '+ref('calendar')+'</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Three years, TTM and cash conversion</h2><p>USD million. Annual financial statements and SEC interim statements are used rather than scanner ratios. TTM2026 ends September 30, 2026; TTM2025 ends September 30, 2025. TTM = preceding fiscal year + current nine months − prior nine months. '+ref('annual')+'; '+ref('q3')+'; '+ref('prior')+'</p>'+table(['Period','Revenue','Reported EBIT','Net income','CFO','Capex','CFO − capex','D&A','SBC'],[[p]+[f(a[p][k]) for k in ['revenue','ebit','net_income','cfo','capex','cfo_less_capex','da','sbc']] for p in ['FY2023','FY2024','FY2025','TTM2025','TTM2026']]))
R.append('<p>TTM revenue increased '+f((a['TTM2026']['revenue']/a['TTM2025']['revenue']-1)*100)+'%, while reported EBIT changed '+f((a['TTM2026']['ebit']/a['TTM2025']['ebit']-1)*100)+'% and net income '+f((a['TTM2026']['net_income']/a['TTM2025']['net_income']-1)*100)+'%. Revenue growth across 2024–25 is not purely organic: the Weener acquisition closed in October 2024, with a $921.563m net cash acquisition outflow. It contributes a full year in 2025 against a partial year in 2024. Do not project the acquisition step-up indefinitely. '+ref('annual')+'</p><p>Company FCF for 2025 was $445.187m: CFO $729.841m less capex $307.092m, plus asset proceeds $10.040m and outstanding-check changes $12.398m. Plain CFO less capex was $422.749m. In 2024, the negative outstanding-check change lowered company FCF below plain CFO less capex. These definitions are not interchangeable and neither is unlevered FCFF. '+ref('q4')+'</p><p>Seasonality is major: nine-month 2026 CFO was −$646.363m, while Q4 2025 alone generated $1,293.806m CFO. Accounts receivable, pack-season inventory and supplier payments drive timing. The filing describes historic peak seasonal working-capital requirements averaging approximately $600m. A simple annualization of nine-month CFO is invalid; a normalized annual cash forecast paired with unreleased peak-season debt is also not a complete fair-value bridge. The explicit seasonal sensitivity below is kept separate from recurring growth reinvestment. '+ref('q3')+'</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Last four quarters and comparable periods</h2>'+table(['Quarter','Revenue / prior','EBIT / prior','Net income / prior','CFO / prior','Capex / prior'],[[q['period']]+[f(q[k])+' / '+f(q['prior_'+k]) for k in ['revenue','ebit','net_income','cfo','capex']] for q in x['quarters']])+'<p>Q4 is fiscal year minus nine months. Q2 and Q3 cash flow are cumulative-period differences. Q1/Q2 releases round to $0.1m; the reconstruction ties to the exact TTM within $0.2m. Do not present these rounded quarter cash flows as exact to the dollar. '+ref('q1')+'; '+ref('q2')+'; '+ref('q4')+'; '+ref('q3')+'</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Normalize earnings without making cash disappear</h2>'+table(['TTM EBIT to recurring EBITA','USD m'],[[k.replace('_',' '),f(v)] for k,v in x['ebita_normalization'].items()]+[['Model recurring EBITA',f(x['normalized_ebita'])]])+'<p>The $35m recurring restructuring allowance is an analyst assumption, below the 2023–25 average of $42.8m because the prior program was described as substantially completed, but not zero: rationalization is a repeated cost of this operating model. Test the full historical run rate before a target. The forecast contains no future acquisitions or unfinanced acquired growth. Transaction fees are isolated rather than treated as an indefinitely recurring organic expense; the deal model must separately fund all closing/integration fees.</p><p>SBC remains an operating cost: the $21.889m TTM charge is not added back. Diluted shares are modeled at 106.5m, above the Q3 weighted average 105.943m and October 31 actual 105.682m, as a modest dilution assumption, not a reported fully diluted point-in-time count. The authorized $500m repurchase program is not assumed executed. '+ref('q3')+'</p><p>Acquired amortization is removed from EBITA, then excluded from the D&A add-back. Only a declining assumed cash-tax shield is added, reaching zero by year 10. This is an assumption about tax deductibility/timing, not a verified universal deduction. Software capital expenditure/depreciation is not separately quantified in these schedules; it is left within reported aggregate capital spending and depreciation, never set to zero or added twice. Depreciation is $267.967m TTM after subtracting acquired amortization from D&A. Maintenance capital starts near depreciation and rises to 105% by year 10, unchanged in terminal years. Growth capex is additional.</p><p>The 26% normalized operating tax rate avoids extrapolating 2024’s 20.7% effective rate, benefited by foreign tax restructuring/reserve reversals, or 2025’s 30.2%, affected by non-deductible rationalization. Cash taxes paid differ from expense: $103.678m in FY2025 and $111.302m in nine-month 2026. Inventory-layer gains are removed once from forward earnings; there is no second $34m equity or cash deduction. No unsupported amount is assigned to incremental LIFO cash taxes. '+ref('annual')+'; '+ref('q3')+'</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Conditional ten-year FCFF diagnostic</h2><p><strong>These are legacy-business stress calculations, not SLGN’s current fair value.</strong> They begin with TTM recurring EBITA and the September balance sheet, use generic year 1–10 annual periods, and deliberately exclude the pending transaction. They have no verified stub-period cash bridge to November 6. Results must not populate the current intrinsic-value or margin-of-safety field.</p><p>For every forecast year and terminal year: operating NOPAT = EBITA × (1 − 26%). Net growth investment = max(positive ΔNOPAT / incremental ROIC, positive Δrevenue / sales-to-capital ratio + ΔNWC). Thus margin improvement consumes capital too. ΔNWC = positive Δrevenue × normalized working-capital ratio; it is included once. Growth capex = net growth investment − ΔNWC. Total capex = replacement maintenance + growth capex. FCFF = operating NOPAT + declining amortization tax shield + depreciation − total capex − ΔNWC − the separate Central States payment. There is no terminal reinvestment holiday.</p>'+table(['Case','Revenue growth Y1–5','Margin Y5','ROIC','Sales/capital','NWC / sales','WACC','Terminal growth'],[[k.title(),f(c['assumptions']['growth']*100)+'%',f(c['assumptions']['margin']*100)+'%',f(c['assumptions']['roic']*100)+'%',f(c['assumptions']['sales_capital'])+'x',f(c['assumptions']['nwc']*100)+'%',f(c['assumptions']['wacc']*100)+'%',f(c['assumptions']['terminal_g']*100)+'%'] for k,c in x['cases'].items()])+'<p>Growth fades over years 6–10 to the terminal rate; EBITA margins reach the scenario level in year 5. The base assumes moderate organic demand and cost recovery, not the 2025 acquisition jump; the bull requires sustained dispensing mix improvement. ROIC 9–15% is a scenario assumption, not a measured current company ROIC. Working-capital floors 7–10% and sales/capital 2.2–2.8x prevent free growth in a manufacturing business. The physical-capacity floor conservatively funds all nominal revenue growth; actual pass-through inflation may need less physical capacity, which is a reason to test the sales/capital assumption rather than quietly remove the floor.</p>')
R.append('<p>Discount-rate calibration: approximately 4.8–4.9% Treasury rates; assumed 5% equity risk premium and roughly 1.0 operating/equity beta imply about 9.8% cost of equity. A forward 6–7% borrowing cost is more relevant than legacy 2.25–4.25% euro note coupons. An illustrative 65/35 equity/debt mix gives about 8% before integration/model-risk allowance; base 9%, bear 10%, bull 8% bracket that judgment. Neither beta, target capital mix nor borrowing spread is a verified market estimate. '+ref('rates')+'; '+ref('q3')+'</p>'+table(['Case','EV','Equity residual / share, unreleased seasonal balance','Terminal share of EV','Terminal EV / EBITDA'],[[k.title(),f(c['enterprise_value']),'≤ $0' if c['conditional_value_per_share']<0 else '$'+f(c['conditional_value_per_share']),f(c['terminal_ev_share']*100)+'%',f(c['terminal_ev_ebitda'])+'x'] for k,c in x['cases'].items()])+'<p>A negative arithmetic equity residual in the bear case is floored at zero for common equity; it is not a forecast of insolvency. The base result is heavily affected by the conservative capital funding and seasonal balance pairing. Do not interpret the large difference from the scanner price as a short recommendation.</p>')
R.append('<h3>Base operating forecast, USD million</h3>'+table(['Year','Revenue','EBITA','NOPAT','Tax shield','Depreciation','Maintenance','Growth capex','ΔNWC','FCFF'],[[str(r['year']) if r['year']<=10 else 'Terminal']+[f(r[k]) for k in ['revenue','ebita','nopat','amort_tax_shield','depreciation','maintenance_capex','growth_capex','delta_nwc','fcff']] for r in b['years']])+'<p>The Central States payment is conservatively held at $2.6m even in the perpetuity, although disclosed payments end in 2040. This modest downward bias should be removed in a date-matched final model; it cannot rescue a missing transaction bridge.</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Balance sheet, sensitivity and what the price asks</h2><p>Debt principal is $4,562.071m, not the $4,547.999m carrying value net of issuance costs. It already includes $35.701m finance leases. Cash is $324.989m; only $174.989m is treated as excess after an assumed $150m operating reserve. Operating-lease rent stays in EBITA and operating-lease liabilities are not deducted again. FY2025 unfunded international pension liabilities of $91.019m and other postretirement liabilities of $8.583m are subtracted; inaccessible domestic pension surpluses are not credited. The foreign deficit is carried from the last full annual disclosure and needs updating; foreign pension service cost remains in earnings, with no duplicate forecast deficit contribution. Central States payments are modeled in FCFF and its liability is not subtracted again. '+ref('q3')+'; '+ref('annual')+'</p>'+table(['Additional realized seasonal cash release','Bear / share','Base / share','Bull / share'],[[k]+[('≤ $0 (arithmetic −$0.00485)' if v[c]<0 else '$'+f(v[c])) for c in ['bear','base','bull']] for k,v in x['seasonal_release_sensitivity'].items()])+'<p>The $600m case corresponds to the disclosed historical average peak requirement, not a forecast of realizable September-to-November cash. $1,000m is a sensitivity, not an asserted fact. Supplier-finance obligations classified in trade accounts payable were $482.0m at September 2026, $385.8m at September 2025 and $438.5m at December 2025. The filing keeps payments in operating cash flow under existing supplier terms. These are not automatically additional debt, and the $96.2m year-over-year increase is not the change in total operating cash flow. A withdrawal or payment-term contraction could accelerate working-capital cash needs: an illustrative 25% unwind of the September balance is $120.5m, or $1.13/share before offsets, not an asserted liability reclassification or forecast. '+ref('q3')+' Every $100m net cash/claim error moves the conditional residual by $0.94/share. Do not both add this cash and count the same working-capital release in FCFF.</p>'+table(['WACC','Terminal growth','Conditional base residual / share'],[[f(v['wacc']*100)+'%',f(v['g']*100)+'%','$'+f(v['value'])] for v in x['sensitivity']])+'<p>At the independently verified November 6 close of $41.09, the conservative unreleased-balance base funding framework requires approximately '+f(x['inverse']['required_y5_ebita_margin_no_seasonal_release']*100)+'% EBITA margin from year 5 at 3% early revenue growth, 9% WACC and 12% incremental ROIC. That is a mechanical inverse diagnostic, not the market’s unique implied forecast: slower capital requirements, seasonal cash realization, a different discount rate and the proposed transaction can all explain part of the gap. It does show that the current legacy base assumptions alone do not establish a margin of safety.</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Trivium: the bridge that must be built</h2><p>The announced transaction values Trivium at approximately $3.4bn EV, or 7.1x LTM adjusted EBITDA before synergies. Contributed Silgan metal/flat-cap businesses are valued at $3.8bn EV, or 8.1x. AIP contributes approximately $1bn for 40%; Silgan retains 60% and receives approximately $2.3bn cash intended to retire debt. MCC will be fully consolidated and has committed approximately $4.9bn term loans plus a $1bn revolving facility. The latter is capacity, not all drawn debt. The $3.1bn assumed Trivium obligations must be reconciled to the new financing, not blindly added again. '+ref('deal')+'; '+ref('q3')+'</p><p>At the announced approximate valuations, $1bn/40% suggests $2.5bn total partnership equity; $7.2bn combined contribution/acquisition EV less that equity suggests roughly $4.7bn net debt before financing fees, cash and other adjustments. This is only a reasonableness bridge, not an exact closing capitalization. It is directionally consistent with substantial new MCC financing. Treating the $2.3bn receipt as free debt relief while retaining 100% of existing earnings and all synergies would overstate value.</p><p>A defensible close-case equity calculation is: value remaining Dispensing Solutions FCFF minus its allocated net debt, plus 60% × (MCC enterprise value − MCC net debt − other senior claims), minus parent/integration costs not already modeled. Alternatively consolidate 100% and subtract the fair value of the 40% minority. Do one, not both. The carved-out flat-cap business crosses current segment boundaries, so the old three-segment statements are insufficient for a clean current target without a carve-out bridge.</p><p>Management expects $75m/$150m/$225m annual synergies in years 1/2/3 and at least 15% adjusted EPS and FCF accretion at full run rate. These are claims, not achieved free cash. Incremental capex, restructuring cash costs, stranded overhead, tax, financing costs and antitrust remedies must be financed. A no-close/delay scenario is essential. The November 6 lender consent permits guarantor and equity-pledge releases upon the entities’ unrestricted designation, subject to the stated conditions. Business contributions are restricted to reasonably prior to, or in connection with, the transaction; designation reverses if the transaction is terminated. The consent also permits specified debt-redemption use of proceeds. It is progress, not proof that all releases or regulatory closing conditions are complete. '+ref('deal')+'; '+ref('consent')+'</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Peer and terminal checks</h2><p>Crown is a useful metal-container comparator but is more beverage-can exposed; its Q3 global beverage volumes grew 6%, contrasting with Silgan metal volumes −4%. Crown’s secondary trailing EV/EBITDA screen is 8.71x at the checked November 6 close, not a normalized synchronized target. Aptar is a better dispensing comparator but has a different pharma mix and economics; its Q3 core sales rose 5% and adjusted EBITDA margin was 21.3%, versus 23.2% prior. Do not import Aptar margins across all of Silgan. '+ref('crown')+'; '+ref('crownmultiple')+'; '+ref('aptar')+'</p><p>The diagnostic terminal EV/EBITDA range 4.9–8.4x is below or near these transaction/metal-packaging checks rather than assuming a high-growth rerating. The low bear multiple is a stress result of higher discount and reinvestment burden; it is not an observed peer quote. Public multiples use distinct EBITDA definitions and ownership/debt conventions. A homogeneous multi-year historical valuation-multiple series has not been verified. The spot peer and announced deal checks do not establish Silgan’s historical premium or discount. No P/E or FCF multiple substitutes for the FCFF model.</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>What proves or kills the thesis</h2><ul><li>Prove: dispensing demand and mix improve without relying on pass-through; metal volumes stabilize; earnings hold after removing new inventory-layer benefits.</li><li>Prove: fourth-quarter receivables collections and normalized inventory turn convert profit into cash without unusually extended supplier financing or outstanding checks.</li><li>Prove: verified carve-out FCFF, closing debt/cash/minority bridge and costed integration plan support value above price under non-bull assumptions.</li><li>Kill/reassess: persistent volume declines and delayed manufacturing-cost recovery, cash conversion below normalized expectations, larger integration spend, adverse approval remedies or a materially more expensive capital stack.</li><li>Entry remains pending: current OHLC history and a refreshed regular-session price when considering entry, EMA trends, support, confirmation method, stop, resistance, account/risk capacity and an approved current valuation.</li></ul><h3>Exact gaps versus ordinary assumptions</h3><p>Missing decision-critical work: deal carve-out financials and fully allocated after-financing ownership value; verified cash/debt and seasonal bridge to the valuation date; final tax treatment, integration cost/capex and financing details; technical trend and entry inputs; the November 6 regular close is independently verified. These gaps prevent a current fair value, margin of safety or entry level. No operation was placed.</p><p>Ordinary scenario assumptions, not missing historical data: future growth, normalized restructuring allowance, margins, tax rate, capital productivity, incremental ROIC, maintenance replacement cost, NWC floor, excess-cash reserve, dilution and discount/terminal rates. Those can and should be varied; substituting assumptions for actual undisclosed transaction amounts must remain explicit.</p><p>Evidence confidence: high for directly read filing facts and accounting identity checks; medium for rounded quarter reconstruction; conditional for management outlook. Underwriting readiness: provisional, not decision-ready. Next work is the deal-inclusive operating/capital-stack bridge and current seasonal reconciliation, not another generic company summary.</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Resumen y conclusión / Summary and conclusion</h2><p><strong>ES:</strong> SLGN queda en vigilancia, sin valor razonable vigente, margen de seguridad ni entrada aprobada. El 10-Q cuantifica beneficios de liquidación LIFO de $26,8m en el trimestre y $34,0m en nueve meses. La operación Trivium exige valorar nueva deuda, costes de integración y una participación minoritaria del 40%; el FCFF del negocio anterior no es un objetivo actual. '+ref('q3')+'; '+ref('deal')+'</p><p><strong>EN:</strong> SLGN remains watchlist-only, without a current fair value, margin of safety or approved entry. The 10-Q quantifies LIFO liquidation benefits of $26.8m in the quarter and $34.0m over nine months. Trivium requires new debt, integration costs and 40% minority ownership in the valuation; legacy-business FCFF is not a current target. '+ref('q3')+'; '+ref('deal')+'</p><p>Both captions are analytical summaries of the linked primary filings, not company quotations. Reported figures are source facts; the watchlist conclusion is the analyst judgment.</p></section>')
R.append('<section><h2>Evidence excerpts</h2><p>Short contiguous quotations, with the source location needed to verify the complete context.</p><ul><li>10-Q Note 5, Inventories: “decreasing cost of goods sold by $26.8 million and $34.0 million” ('+ref('q3')+'). The surrounding sentence identifies three- and nine-month 2026 periods.</li><li>Transaction release, capitalization: “the partnership will establish an independent balance sheet that will be fully consolidated into Silgan’s financial statements” ('+ref('deal')+').</li><li>November 6 consent: “such entity will be released as a guarantor under the Credit Agreement” ('+ref('consent')+').</li></ul><h3>Source ledger</h3>'+''.join('<p>'+ref(k)+' · '+v['date']+' · '+v['purpose']+'</p>' for k,v in s.items())+'</section>')
css='body{font:16px/1.65 system-ui,sans-serif;color:#193044;background:#eef3f4;margin:0}main{max-width:1100px;margin:auto;background:white;padding:48px}header{border-bottom:5px solid #0a7774;padding-bottom:28px}h1{font-size:42px;line-height:1.15;color:#12363c}h2{margin-top:42px;color:#075d5b}h3{margin-top:28px}.eyebrow{letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:#397271;font-size:13px}.lead{font-size:21px}section{border-bottom:1px solid #dbe6e6;padding-bottom:18px}a{color:#086d7d}table{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13px}th,td{text-align:right;padding:9px;border-bottom:1px solid #d8e1e4;white-space:nowrap}th:first-child,td:first-child{text-align:left}th{background:#e7f0f1}.scroll{overflow-x:auto}strong{color:#8a4611}@media(max-width:700px){main{padding:22px}h1{font-size:32px}}'
(P/'report.html').write_text('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>SLGN | Cash quality and Trivium bridge</title><style>'+css+'</style><main>'+''.join(R)+'</main></html>')
# Machine captions are extracted from the report's substantive bilingual cards.
import re
page=(P/'report.html').read_text()
plain=html.unescape(re.sub('<[^>]+>','',page))
captions={}
for language,label in [('es','ES'),('en','EN')]:
    match=re.search(r'<strong>'+label+r':</strong> (.*?) <a href=',page)
    assert match, 'Missing bilingual report caption: '+label
    captions[language]=html.unescape(re.sub('<[^>]+>','',match.group(1)))
    assert captions[language] in plain, 'Caption is not contiguous in decoded report'
captions['source_urls']=[s['q3']['url'],s['deal']['url']]
captions['provenance']='Analyst summaries of linked primary filings. Reported financial and transaction figures are source facts; the watchlist conclusion is analyst judgment. Not company quotations.'
for url in captions['source_urls']:
    assert url in page, 'Summary evidence URL absent from report'
(P/'summary-es-en.json').write_text(json.dumps(captions,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
print('Report generated')
